assertion  XSH.remquo.001
anchor     functions/remquo.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/remquo.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       remquo(x, y, &q) returns the value remainder(x, y) gives, for the
           pairs (5, 3), (7, 2), (5, 2), (-5, 2), (1, 4), (3, 4), (6, 3), (1.5,
           1), (-1.5, 1), (2.5, 1), (-7, 2) and (5.5, 2).
test       t/001-remainder.c

assertion  XSH.remquo.002
anchor     functions/remquo.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/remquo.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       For x/y equal to 2.25, -2.25, -2.25, 2.25, 5, -5, 3.4, 14.28, -14.28
           and -14.28 (pairs 9/4, -9/4, 9/-4, -9/-4, 10/2, -10/2, 17/5, 100/7,
           -100/7, 100/-7), the stored value has the sign of x/y and its
           magnitude agrees with the quotient (2, 2, 2, 2, 5, 5, 3, 14, 14, 14)
           in the low three bits (the modulus 2^n has n at least 3).
note       Q-104: only pairs whose quotient has a fractional part under one half
           are used, so the result does not depend on whether "integral quotient"
           means the rounded or the truncated quotient.
test       t/002-quotient.c

assertion  XSH.remquo.003
anchor     functions/remquo.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/remquo.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       remquo(NaN, 2, &q), remquo(2, NaN, &q) and remquo(NaN, NaN, &q) return
           a NaN.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/003-nan.c

assertion  XSH.remquo.004
anchor     functions/remquo.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/remquo.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/remquo.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2001
says       remquo(5.5, 0, &q), remquo(0, 0, &q), remquo(Inf, 2, &q) and
           remquo(-Inf, 2, &q) return a NaN and are domain errors, reported
           through errno (EDOM) and the invalid exception as math_errhandling
           selects.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/004-domain.c

assertion  XSH.remquo.005
anchor     functions/remquo.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/remquo.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
since      2001
says       With errno holding EDOM before the call, remquo(5.5, 2, &q) leaves it
           unchanged and, when exception flags are in use, raises none of the
           invalid, divide-by-zero and overflow exceptions.
test       t/005-no-error.c
